This leaf is not a Gauss manuscript. It is a printed page: page 3, signature 'A 2', of an 18th-century German elementary mathematics textbook set in Fraktur. It has been bound into or photographed with the bundle. It contains no derivation or computation.

The text has two parts.

(1) The end of an introductory section on the method of mathematical exposition, in the Wolffian 'mathematical method' style. It explains the terms:
- immediate consequences of theorems: Zusätze / Corollaria;
- applications so easy they need no further instruction: Foderungssätze / Postulata;
- applications that do need instructions: Auflösung / solutio, which must also come with a proof that the construction satisfies the requirement;
- useful material that cannot be fitted into the strict chain of reasoning: Anmerkungen / Scholia;
- propositions borrowed from other sciences: Lehn-Sätze / Lemmata, said to be rarer in mathematics than elsewhere;
- experience, which in applied mathematics often stands in for proved propositions;
- arbitrary assumptions: Hypotheses, admissible only for convenience and without harm to truth.

It then makes a pedagogical remark. Mathematics starts from sensory representations and moves gradually to abstract intellectual concepts, which makes it the best means of training youth to think.

(2) The start of 'Erster Abschnitt. Rechnung mit einfachen Zahlen. 1) Methoden, die Größe als Zahl auszudrucken'. §3 gives the definitions:
- Größe (magnitude) is whatever can be increased or decreased.
- A magnitude is homogeneous ('gleichartig') if its parts can be determined by one and the same measure.
- Examples are lengths in foot-measure: 2 Fuß = 1 Elle; 10 or 12 Fuß = 1 Ruthe. The sentence continues on p. 4: a Zoll is 1/10 or 1/12 of a Fuß.

The next page continues with the concepts of unit, integer, fraction and common measure. Its worked example has a measure F contained 2, 3, 4 and 5 times in the parts DC, DE, EB and AC of a line AB. So AB = 14 F (my sum; the page itself does not state it).

The handwriting at the left edge of the image is not marginalia on this page. It is the right edge of the facing handwritten leaf (the previous page). That leaf is a bibliography of Euler's papers: Misc. Berol., Mém. Berlin, Acta Erud., Journal littéraire, Misc. Taurin. The fragments match the previous-page transcription line for line ('...rentialium', '...dmittunt', '...une loi e...', '...ranlemen...', '...térieure').
